course: language
lesson: 32
title: Sign language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sign languages are full, real languages made with the hands, face and body, with their own words and their own grammar, as rich as any spoken language.
explain: At a club Lily watches Sam, a Deaf boy, sign to his mum so fast her eyes can't keep up; it isn't English spelled on the hands, it's British Sign Language, a different language altogether.
explain: There is not one sign language for the whole world: Britain has BSL, America has ASL, Ukraine has Ukrainian Sign Language, and even countries that share English have very different sign languages.
explain: Faces matter as much as hands: raised eyebrows can turn a sentence into a question, so a signer's expressions are grammar, not rudeness.
explain: Babies who see signing even "babble" with their hands, which shows that language lives in the brain, not just in the mouth.
fact: British Sign Language (BSL) is a full language with its own grammar, different from English; it uses hand shapes, movements, the face and the body.
fact: People who are part of Deaf culture and use sign language often write Deaf with a capital D.
fact: American Sign Language (ASL) and BSL are different languages; ASL is related to French Sign Language.
fact: BSL fingerspelling uses two hands, ASL fingerspelling uses one; fingerspelling is for names and words without a sign.
fact: In sign languages, facial expressions are part of the grammar: raised eyebrows can turn a sentence into a yes/no question.
fact: In 2022 a new British law said that BSL is a real language of England, Scotland and Wales.
fact: BSL, Auslan (Australian Sign Language) and New Zealand Sign Language belong to one family, so their signers understand each other quite well.
fact: Studies, such as one by Laura-Ann Petitto in 1991, found that babies who see sign language make babbling movements with their hands.
fact: In BSL a simple "hello" is a wave; when talking to a Deaf person, face them, speak clearly if they lip-read, and don't shout.
fact: Sign language interpreters translate live between spoken language and sign language, so Deaf people can follow things like the news.
fact: Ukrainian Sign Language is a separate language, so a Deaf boy from Ukraine moving to London needs to learn BSL, just as hearing children learn English.
exercise: Lily sees Sam signing fast to his mum. Is it English on the hands? | No, BSL is its own language with its own grammar | Yes, every sign stands for one English word, in the same order as speech | Yes, it's English spelled letter by letter | No, it's just mime with no rules
exercise: What does BSL stand for? | British Sign Language | Basic Sign Letters | Big Sound Language | British School Language
exercise: Ava learns BSL and her American friend learns ASL. Can they understand each other easily? | No, they are different languages | Yes, both are English | Yes, all sign languages are the same | Only on Tuesdays
exercise: Which sign language is ASL related to? | French Sign Language | British Sign Language | Auslan | Ukrainian Sign Language
exercise: How many hands does BSL fingerspelling use? | Two | One | None | Three
exercise: What is fingerspelling used for? | Names and words without a sign | Every single word | Only numbers | Only swear words
exercise: Rosa sees signers making faces. Are they being rude? | No, facial expressions are part of the grammar | Yes, it's rude to make faces at people while you talk to them | Yes, they are showing anger | No, they are just tired
exercise: In sign language, what can raised eyebrows do? | Turn a sentence into a yes/no question | Mean "I'm angry with you" every single time | Show the sign is spelled wrong | End the conversation
exercise: Max's grandad says sign language isn't a real language. What did a British law say in 2022? | That BSL is a real language | That BSL is banned in schools | That only spoken English counts as a language | That BSL must be the only language in schools
exercise: Ellie's Australian cousin signs Auslan with a BSL user. Why do they understand each other quite well? | Auslan and BSL belong to the same family | All sign languages in the world are identical to each other | They both use American signs | Auslan is English on hands
exercise: Toby learns babies of Deaf parents babble with their hands. What does that show? | Language is in the brain, not just the mouth | Those babies can't hear at all | Hand-babbling is just waving that means nothing at all | Babies copy any movement
exercise: Hamza wants to say hello to a Deaf pupil in BSL. What's simple? | A wave | A thumbs up | Shouting "HELLO" | Clapping twice
exercise: What should you NOT do when talking to a Deaf person? | Shout | Face them | Speak clearly | Learn a few signs
exercise: Mia sees a person signing on the TV news. What are they doing? | Interpreting the news into sign language | Waving to their family | Directing the cameras | Doing a dance
exercise: Danylo knows Ukrainian Sign Language and moves to London. What will he need? | To learn BSL | Nothing, BSL is the same | To learn ASL | To stop signing
test: Your mate goes "sign language is just mime, innit, anyone gets it." What's true? | It's a full language with grammar you have to learn | He's right, it's only mime that anyone can follow without lessons | It's English with hands | It's only for counting
test: Your cousin thinks there's "one world sign language". What would happen if she used BSL in New York? | Many ASL signers wouldn't understand her | Everyone would understand her perfectly, since signs are the same | Her signs would turn into ASL | Nobody uses signs in New York
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "the interpreter on telly is lowkey sus, Deaf people can just read subs." ("Sus" means suspicious; "subs" means subtitles.) What's fairer? | For many Deaf people sign is their first language, so interpreters matter | He's right, interpreters are pointless because every Deaf person reads subtitles easily | Subtitles are only for hearing people | Interpreters are actors
test: Your grandad shouts at a Deaf neighbour to be heard. What's better? | Face her, speak clearly, and don't shout | Shout even louder | Write very fast | Turn away and talk
test: In a cartoon, a signer keeps a totally blank face while asking a question. What's missing? | Facial grammar, like raised eyebrows for a question | Nothing, because faces don't matter at all in signing | A hat | A second person
test: Max's grandad from Cornwall says "'tis just waving about, my 'ansum." ("My 'ansum" is a friendly word, like dear.) What's the fair answer? | BSL is a real language, recognised in UK law | He's right, it's waving | It's only for children | It's a kind of dance
test: Your American cousin fingerspells with one hand and says you're doing it wrong with two. Who's right? | Both: ASL uses one hand and BSL uses two | Only your cousin, because one hand is the world rule | Only you | Neither, you must use your feet
test: What would happen if a Deaf baby only ever saw sign language at home? | She would learn sign language, even babbling with her hands | She would never learn any language at all, because language needs sounds | She would learn spoken English by herself | She would only learn to wave
test: Your Kiwi mate says "sweet as, my NZ signs work with BSL heaps." ("Sweet as" means great; "heaps" means a lot.) Is he right? | Yes, NZ Sign Language, BSL and Auslan are one family | No, they're totally unrelated | No, NZ has no sign language | Yes, because NZ uses ASL
test: Danylo's new classmates say "just sign in Ukrainian, we'll get it." What's the kind and true answer? | Ukrainian Sign Language is different; help him learn BSL and respect his own | All signs are the same everywhere, so Ukrainian signs will work fine in London | He should stop signing | They can guess every sign
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises British Sign Language? | A complete language possessing its own grammar, distinct from English | A manual representation of English words, presented strictly in English order | A system of mime devoid of grammatical rules | A dialect of American Sign Language
exam: Identify the function performed by raised eyebrows in sign languages. | The marking of a yes/no question as a grammatical feature | The expression of rudeness towards the interlocutor | The indication of a spelling error | The termination of the conversation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning British and American Sign Languages. | They constitute distinct languages, the latter being related to French Sign Language | They constitute one and the same language, notwithstanding minor differences of accent | They are mutually intelligible in all respects | Both derive from Auslan
exam: In respect of fingerspelling, ascertain the number of hands employed in British and in American Sign Language respectively. | Two in British, one in American | One in British, two in American | Two in both languages alike | One in both languages alike
